+  <section id="beat-09-three-paths" class="clip" data-beat-id="beat-09-three-paths" data-start="188" data-duration="27" data-track-index="1"><p class="narration">The last piece is choosing which bullets to bring forward, based on your Next Move Statement. Think about your three possible paths. For a promotion in your current lane, prioritize bullets that show leadership, scope, and strategic decisions—the ones that say "I'm already operating at the next level." For a lateral move, highlight transferable skills and cross-functional projects—"I can bring my skills into new contexts." For a redesign of your current role, focus on process improvements and innovation—"I've already started redesigning my role; now I'm formalizing it."</p><div class="eyebrow">Choose for your next move</div><div class="paths"><div class="path"><h2>↑ Promotion</h2><p>Leadership, scope, and strategic decisions.</p><b>Already at the next level.</b></div><div class="path"><h2>→ Lateral Move</h2><p>Transferable skills and cross-functional work.</p><b>Ready for a new context.</b></div><div class="path"><h2>↗ Role Redesign</h2><p>Process improvement and innovation.</p><b>Formalize what you started.</b></div></div></section>
+  <section id="beat-10-headline-stories" class="clip" data-beat-id="beat-10-headline-stories" data-start="215" data-duration="17" data-track-index="1"><p class="narration">Don't try to perfect everything at once. Select the three to five strongest bullets that align with your chosen path, reflect your true impact, and help others see the future you from your Next Move Statement. Those become your headline stories.</p><div class="eyebrow">Do less · choose better</div><div class="number">3–5</div><div class="title">headline stories</div><div class="subtitle">Aligned with your path. True to your impact. Pointing to future you.</div></section>
+  <section id="beat-11-linkedin-visibility" class="clip" data-beat-id="beat-11-linkedin-visibility" data-start="232" data-duration="16" data-track-index="1"><p class="narration">The same principles apply to your LinkedIn profile, with one addition: LinkedIn is searchable and public, so it's also a visibility tool. The words in your headline and About section decide whether the right people—including the opportunity holders you'll meet in Module 4—find you at all.</p><div class="eyebrow">Resume evidence → public visibility</div><div class="title">Help the right people<br>find future you.</div><div class="checklist"><div class="check"><b>Headline</b>Searchable direction</div><div class="check"><b>About</b>Credible story</div><div class="check"><b>Module 4</b>Opportunity holders</div></div></section>
+  <section id="beat-12-headline" class="clip" data-beat-id="beat-12-headline" data-start="248" data-duration="16" data-track-index="1"><p class="narration">Your headline is the most-read line you own. Most headlines just state a current title; yours should point at future you. Paste your current headline, plus a sentence describing your next move if you have one, and ask the AI to rewrite it so it credibly signals where you're heading—without overclaiming. Then choose the version that feels both ambitious and true. That final judgment call is yours.</p><div class="eyebrow">The most-read line you own</div><div class="headline strike">Current title</div><div class="arrow">↓</div><div class="headline after">Credibly signal where you’re heading.</div><div class="subtitle">Ambitious and true. The final judgment is yours.</div></section>
+</main>
